    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d284">

                <pb facs="0493" n="379" xml:id="pg379"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/1424a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Senator Dwight W. Morrow</hi><anchor n="88"
                        xml:id="d284-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-13">March 13, 1931</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Henderson desires that we should be represented in a drafting committee to put in
                    shape the recent naval agreement. I do not wish to interfere with your vacation
                    by asking you to assume that position but if you would be willing to go to
                    London on your arrival in Europe long enough to explain my views thoroughly to
                    Henderson, Malkin<anchor n="89" xml:id="d284-f2"/> and Dawes, it might then be
                    possible to have the work carried on by others. Your recollection as to my views
                    can be refreshed by recent cables to Dawes. Your influence in directing this
                    into expeditious and safe channels so far as American ratification is concerned
                    would be most helpful and I cannot think of any other person who could take your
                    place. I might ask Rublee<anchor n="90" xml:id="d284-f3"/> to come over later to
                    carry on your suggestions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d284-f1" n="88" type="source" xml:id="d284-f1-content">
                <p>Senator from New Jersey; he was on board the S. S. <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Leviathan</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d284-f2" n="89" xml:id="d284-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Herbert William Malkin, legal adviser to the British Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d284-f3" n="90" xml:id="d284-f3-content">
                <p>George Rublee, adviser to the American delegation to the London Naval Conference,
                    1930.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
